Let's say you work a little _harder_ at granting ne
that.  You seem still stuck with the delusion that
the only way to be entertained by a game is to win
its ultimate goal.

That is false.

Millions of kids play soccer with no reasonable
expectation of ending up as the next Pele, carried
off the field in triumph for scoring the goal that
wins the World's Cup, arguably that game's highest
possible achievement.

They manage to have a wonderful time, anyway.

Lots of NetHack players are _delighted_ when they
can reach DLVL 5 in safety with a PC, me for one.

Solving Socoban, safely cleaning out Mines End,
playing the protection racket with success, are
all entertaining goals in and of themselves.

"All or nothing" is a _terrible_ philosophy for a
game designer to take. No successful ones chose that
approach, now do they?

> If I make a platform jumping game, should I bow to
> those who don't want to jump platforms?  If I make
> a "Dive the dungeon and get the foozle" game,
> should I bow to those who don't want to be
> bothered fetching the foozle?

Only if you want to end up the next Myst
millionnaire.

Back when Doom 1 was the hottest thing, a grandneice
of mine figured out how to insert the CD-ROM, play
the game, and advance a few levels, at the ripe old
age of two years. She had no hope of "winning" that
game, yet the game designers had catered to the fact
that for many, winning is neither possible nor
necessary, and so the game was entertaining _for
her_, a grand commentary on the kind of excellent
design that funds tall office buildings in Silicon
Valley.

You want to deprive the DevTeam of the use of that
insight that "winning need not be the only goal",
for whatever agenda-laden reasons of your own.

You attempt to enforce your viewpoint by joining
those screaming about all the "unjust ascensions"
the pudding farmers, altar squatters, credit
cloners, and other folks who play the game in styles
other than yours might find entertaining, and that
just stinks, both from a game design point of view,
and from a personal morality point of view.

